{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000452",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches on trait_efo (basal cell carcinoma). Using endpoint fidelity plus comparable performance among models with basic demographic/PC covariates, PGS000452 (GWAS Hits, 28 variants; MGI development; European evaluation) has the strongest visible validation performance in this set: full-model AUROC=0.632 (0.616–0.647) with Nagelkerke R2=0.0487 and OR per SD=1.663 (1.57–1.761), and a strong top-1% vs rest OR=3.61. Very similar alternatives exist (e.g., PGS000454 also AUROC=0.632 with nearly identical covariates and effect size), making the top choice not uniquely separated; several UKB-developed variants have larger validation n (~60k) but lower AUROC (~0.611–0.617). Confidence is therefore moderate because the decision hinges on small metric differences within a near-tied cluster and the reported AUROC is full-model (not explicitly PGS-only)."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004944",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 15 candidates are direct matches to glaucoma or primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG), with POAG being a reasonable endpoint-aligned instantiation for a generic glaucoma target. Among candidates with strong, comparable evidence, PGS004944 stands out for (i) clear endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: primary open-angle glaucoma, self-reported), (ii) very large validation support (n=407,667) with multi-ancestry evaluation, and (iii) supportive performance/effect-size signals (full-model AUROC=0.748 and per-SD OR=1.74). However, the selection remains sub-optimal because many models report only full-model AUROC (not PRS-only) or only effect sizes, making cross-model comparability limited; additionally, at least one older model (PGS000137) reports a higher full-model AUROC=0.80 but with much smaller validation (n=1,795) and covariates including family history (a comparability concern). Given the incomplete PRS-only metric reporting across the set, PGS004944 is preferred on the breadth and consistency of visible evidence, but confidence is not high."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000639",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for Hodgkin(s) lymphoma are present (PGS000637/PGS000638/PGS000639/PGS000640 and PGS003449). Among the models with coherent endpoint alignment (phenotyping_reported = \"Hodgkin's disease\"; trait_efo = \"Hodgkins lymphoma\"), PGS000639 has the strongest reported discrimination and PRS signal in the provided metadata (full_model AUROC 0.62; Nagelkerke pseudo-R² 0.0276; per-SD OR 1.476 [1.154–1.889]) versus the other Cancer PRSweb Hodgkin’s disease scores (AUROC 0.569–0.601; pseudo-R² 0.0109–0.0193). PGS003449 is label-aligned (trait_reported \"Hodgkin lymphoma\") but has contradictory phenotyping_reported (\"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia\") and no AUC/R² reported, reducing its support from visible evidence. Overall quality remains sub-optimal because all the best-aligned Hodgkin’s disease candidates have very small validation samples (n=775) and the reported AUROCs are full-model (not explicitly PGS-only), limiting comparability and certainty."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004695",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001791, PGS004695), both aligned to idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis by trait_reported/trait_efo. On endpoint fidelity, PGS001791 uses a generic IPF phenotype, while PGS004695 is explicitly incident IPF (a mild mismatch for a generic target but still direct-match). The publication context favors PGS004695 as more disease-focused (IPF susceptibility in an exposure/epidemiology setting) versus PGS001791 coming from a pan-trait/global biobank framework paper, which domain guidance suggests down-ranking when a disease-focused alternative exists. Performance evidence is not cleanly comparable: PGS001791 reports a covariates-including AUROC=0.758 plus a small PRS-only/covariates-regressed-out R2=0.005929 (consistent with the low observed-scale heritability sanity-check), whereas PGS004695 lacks AUC/R2 and reports only an HR (high vs low PRS quintile)=3.78. Given the framework-penalty for PGS001791 and the presence of a strong effect-size signal in PGS004695, PGS004695 is preferred, but missing standard discrimination metrics and the incident-only endpoint keep this from being high-quality, yielding low confidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000054",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 5 candidates are direct matches on trait_efo (late-onset Alzheimer's disease). The best-supported option from the visible evidence is PGS000054 because it is endpoint-aligned (phenotyping_reported: familial late-onset Alzheimer's disease), comes from a disease-focused publication (“Polygenic risk scores in familial Alzheimer disease”), uses basic covariates (age, sex), and reports a comparatively strong per-SD effect size in evaluation (OR=1.73, 95% CI 1.57–1.93) with a reasonable validation sample (n=3,324). Limitations: none of the candidates report clearly PRS-comparable AUROC or PRS-only R²; reported metrics are mostly null or non-comparable (e.g., PGS004918 provides only full-model AUROC with very small validation n=136; PGS000334 reports R²=0.191 with unknown covariates, which is difficult to interpret and potentially non-comparable). Also, PGS000054 is evaluated in Hispanic/Latin American ancestry while the target deployment ancestry is unspecified, leaving transportability unresolved and preventing a high-confidence choice."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003578",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present (24 models with trait_efo mapping to major depressive disorder or closely related recurrence specifiers). Several candidates have clear endpoint alignment to MDD, but many have limitations: some are evaluated on non-disease endpoints despite MDD labels (e.g., bilateral putamen volume; cognition/BOLD activation; internalising behavior; antidepressant side-effect phenotype), and some report only full-model metrics or effect sizes without PRS-only discrimination metrics, limiting comparability. Among the most endpoint-faithful options with usable PRS-comparable performance evidence, the 2023 bioRxiv 'Phenotype integration improves power and preserves specificity in biobank-based genetic studies of MDD' family (PGS003576–PGS003585) reports European evaluation and consistent R² values; within this coherent family, PGS003578 shows the highest reported R² (0.0257) with an MDD diagnosis endpoint and basic PC covariates, making it the best-supported choice from the visible evidence. Confidence is not high because reported metrics are not explicitly PRS-only (the R² labeling is generic), covariates are not fully detailed beyond PCs, and other candidates have partially non-comparable metrics (e.g., time-to-event C-index, hazard ratios, incremental R²) that prevent a clean, definitive cross-model ranking."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004563",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches for nephrolithiasis via aligned EFO/diagnostic-code endpoints (N20 calculus of kidney and ureter; or calculus of kidney and ureter including ureterolithiasis). PGS001250 provides the most complete quantitative performance metadata (including PGS-only AUROC/R2 and incremental AUROC) but comes from a clearly pan-trait UKB snpnet framework paper and uses a time-to-event endpoint, both of which are down-ranking signals in the provided domain policy when disease-focused alternatives exist. PGS004493 and PGS004563 are newer, endpoint-faithful N20 scores from the same (non-pan-trait) publication, but they lack comparable AUROC/R2 and have unknown covariates, leaving only per-SD ORs as performance evidence; between them, PGS004563 has the higher reported OR (1.283 vs 1.227), so it is the best-supported choice within that study family. Confidence is low because the top candidate lacks directly comparable discrimination metrics and covariate transparency, and the evidence does not clearly separate it from the snpnet model beyond the framework/time-to-event penalty guidance."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept (nodular goiter) via either nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814/PGS002022) or benign nodular goiter (PGS005261/PGS005262/PGS005263). The two "thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter" scores (PGS005273/PGS005274) include nodular goiter but are contrast endpoints rather than a pure nodular-goiter disease endpoint, so they are less endpoint-faithful for a generic nodular goiter target.

Among the endpoint-faithful candidates, PGS005262 is preferred because it comes from a disease-focused multi-biobank thyroid study (many contributing cohorts listed) and has the strongest reported discrimination within the benign nodular goiter set (full_model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876) alongside a comparatively strong reported per-SD effect size (OR 1.408). In contrast, PGS001814 and PGS002022 are from a UKB portability framework paper and show only modest PRS-comparable partial correlations (0.0277 and 0.024 in EUR), with no AUC/R2 reported.

Limitations: for the 2026 benign nodular goiter models, only full-model AUROC is shown and covariates are listed as unknown, making performance comparability and PRS-only contribution unclear per the provided domain policy; therefore the direct-match selection is not high-confidence and the overall outcome is DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL rather than DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005217",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct/near-direct matches to the target concept (peripheral vascular disease), with two UKB portability-framework scores labeled exactly as peripheral vascular disease (PGS001843, PGS002055) and two disease-focused peripheral artery disease (PAD) scores (PGS005158, PGS005217). PGS005217 is best supported overall because it comes from a disease-focused PAD study with an incident PAD endpoint, very large validation (n=304,294) across multiple ancestries, clean/basic covariates (age, sex, PCs), and reports strong discrimination/effect-size signals (C-index 0.731; HR 1.66). The main limitation is phenotype alignment ambiguity: PAD is closely related but not identical to the broader/unspecified peripheral vascular disease label, and the exact-label alternatives (PGS001843/PGS002055) have only weak PRS-comparable partial-r (~0.015–0.018) from a portability framework, making the overall choice supportive but not definitive."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005217",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are broadly direct-match for the target concept (peripheral vascular disease), with two labeled as peripheral vascular disease unspecified (PGS001843/PGS002055) and two as peripheral artery disease (PGS005158/PGS005217), which is a closely aligned large-vessel subtype. PGS005217 is best supported by the visible evidence because it comes from a disease-focused PAD study context with a very large multi-ancestry evaluation sample (n=304,294) and reports strong time-to-event effect size (HR=1.66, 95% CI 1.61–1.71) plus a reported concordance statistic/full-model C-index of 0.731 using only basic covariates (age, sex, PCs), making its performance signal more interpretable than covariate-heavy models. The main limitation is metric comparability: PGS005217’s reported C-index is a full-model metric (not clearly PRS-only), while the exact-label UKB portability scores (PGS001843/PGS002055) provide only small partial correlations (partial-r ~0.015–0.018) and are framework/portability-derived; the evidence does not include PRS-only AUROC/R2 for a clean head-to-head, so confidence remains moderate rather than high."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003586",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Three direct-match candidates are present. PGS003586 is the most endpoint-faithful to the target trait: trait_reported is “Pre-eclampsia” and phenotyping_reported is “Pre-eclampsia/eclampsia”, from a disease-focused Nat Med study on preeclampsia/gestational hypertension, with basic covariates (maternal age, age^2, PCs). It also shows the strongest reported per-SD effect size among the candidates (OR=1.31, 95% CI 1.24–1.38) versus PGS003587 (OR=1.20, 1.14–1.26) and PGS004593 (HR=1.16, 1.14–1.19). However, all three models lack comparable discrimination metrics (AUC/R2 are null) and PGS004593 has a phenotype inconsistency (trait_reported “Preeclampsia” but phenotyping_reported “Gestational hypertension”), so the selection is evidence-limited and confidence cannot be high."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000611",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches by trait/EFO and largely consistent phenotyping (bladder cancer / cancer of bladder / incident bladder cancer / urothelial carcinoma). Many models report only full-model AUROC (often with age/sex/smoking/BMI covariates) or sparse/indirect metrics, limiting PRS-only comparability; notably, very high full-model AUROC values (e.g., ~0.80 in PGS000071/PGS000782) are not directly interpretable as PRS performance and may be covariate-driven per the provided domain guidance. Among candidates with clearer PRS signal metrics and clean endpoint alignment, the PRSweb GWAS-hits family shows the strongest and most coherent evidence: PGS000611 has aligned phenotyping ('Cancer of bladder'), European evaluation, a reasonable full-model AUROC (0.567) plus supportive PRS effect sizes (OR per SD 1.284, 95% CI 1.211–1.361) and a strong tail-separation metric (OR top 1% vs rest 2.66, 95% CI 1.79–3.93) with a larger training set (UKB; n=12,992) than the otherwise similar MGI-based scores. Remaining limitations are that the reported AUROC is still a full-model metric (not explicitly PGS-only) and several close competitors exist (e.g., PGS000613 with similar AUROC and slightly higher top-1% OR), so the choice is not decisively established from the visible evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000611",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches to urinary bladder cancer (trait/EFO and phenotyping align; several explicitly use “Cancer of bladder” or incident bladder cancer). For selecting the best-supported option, PGS000611 (GWAS Hits; 13 variants) has a clear, PRS-informative performance signal in the provided metadata: full-model AUROC 0.567 (EUR eval) and effect size OR per SD 1.284 (95% CI 1.211–1.361), plus a strong extreme-tail contrast (top 1% vs rest OR 2.66, 95% CI 1.79–3.93) with a reasonable validation size (n=13,530). Competing PRSweb models (e.g., PGS000610/612/613) have similar AUROCs (~0.571–0.572) and similar per-SD ORs (~1.29–1.30), but PGS000611’s tail OR and larger training sample than the MGI-derived PRSweb set make it slightly better supported within that family. The overall outcome is still sub-optimal because many candidates report only full-model AUROC or non-comparable metrics (and some high full-model AUROCs like PGS000071/PGS000782 likely reflect covariate-heavy models), so the evidence does not isolate PRS-only discrimination cleanly across candidates, leaving meaningful ambiguity."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000611",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported bladder cancer; trait_efo urinary bladder carcinoma/cancer; phenotyping_reported bladder cancer/cancer of bladder/incident bladder cancer/urothelial carcinoma). However, most candidates lack PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2), and several report only full-model AUROC that is likely covariate-influenced and non-comparable across studies (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 report ~0.80 AUROC with smoking/BMI; the domain guidance cautions not to treat full-model AUROC as PRS-only). Within the coherent Cancer PRSweb cluster (PGS000607–PGS000616), PGS000611 (GWAS Hits, 13 variants; UKB-developed; EUR eval) shows the strongest visible discriminative evidence among that family: AUROC 0.567 with CI plus a strong top-1% vs rest OR 2.66 (1.79–3.93) and per-SD OR 1.284 (1.211–1.361), with only basic covariates (age/sex/batch/PCs) and a clear bladder cancer endpoint. Competing non-PRSweb scores either have missing/less comparable metrics (e.g., PGS000152, PGS003335, PGS004241) or appear dominated by covariate-heavy full-model discrimination (PGS000071/PGS000782). Remaining limitation: multiple PRSweb candidates are close (e.g., PGS000613 AUROC 0.571; PGS000610 AUROC 0.572) and the context provides no incremental or PRS-only metrics to cleanly separate them, so the selection is best-effort rather than definitive."
}
